---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ShiftSequenceLab from "../../components/guides/ShiftSequenceLab";
import VigenereLab from "../../components/guides/VigenereLab";
import VigenereEffects from "../../components/guides/VigenereEffects";
import KasiskiMini from "../../components/guides/KasiskiMini";
import { ABC, keyLengths, vigenere } from "../../utils/ciphers";
import { PRACTICE } from "../../utils/samples";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const cipher = vigenere(PRACTICE, "MAP", false),
  scores = keyLengths(cipher),
  short = vigenere("MEETATTHEOAKTREE", "MAP", false);
---

<BaseLayout title="Understand and break Vigenère">
  <header class="wrap page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE 03 / FOLLOW THE KEY</p>
    <h1>
      One shift.
      <br />
      Then another<span class="accent">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Start with Caesar, try two alternating shifts, then build a Vigenère key. When the idea makes
      sense, learn three ways to investigate a secret message.
    </p>
  </header>
  <div class="wrap article-layout">
    <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="Guide contents">
      <a href="#caesar">First, remember Caesar</a>
      <a href="#two">Try two shifts</a>
      <a href="#key">Use letters as a key</a>
      <a href="#patterns">Watch the counts change</a>
      <a href="#crib">Guess a word</a>
      <a href="#kasiski">Find repeated groups</a>
      <a href="#ic">Test key lengths with IC</a>
      <a href="#solve">Work a complete example</a>
    </nav>
    <article class="guide-content">
      <h2 id="caesar">First, remember a Caesar shift</h2>
      <p>
        A Caesar cipher slides every letter along the alphabet by the same amount. With a shift of
        7, a becomes H, b becomes I, and e becomes L. If you pass z, keep counting from a again. To
        read the message, slide the letters back by 7.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because every e gets the same replacement, repeated letters stay repeated. The English
        frequency chart keeps its shape and moves around the alphabet. There are only 26 shifts to
        try, so a codebreaker can test all of them fairly quickly.
      </p>
      <ShiftSequenceLab part="caesar" client:load />
      <p class="checkpoint">
        <strong>Before continuing:</strong> with shift 7, the two e’s in “meet” both become L. Can
        you explain why the number of e’s has not been lost, but has moved to the L bar?
      </p>
      <h2 id="two">What if we use two different shifts?</h2>
      <p>
        Suppose we want to hide that simple pattern. We could alternate between two Caesar shifts
        instead of using the same one all the way through:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Letters <strong>1, 3, 5, 7, …</strong> use shift <strong>7</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Letters <strong>2, 4, 6, 8, …</strong> use shift <strong>22</strong>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The first letter uses 7, the second 22, the third 7 again, and so on. The two e’s in “meet”
        now sit at different key positions: one moves 22 places, the other 7. They do not have to
        become the same ciphertext letter.
      </p>
      <ShiftSequenceLab part="two" client:load />
      <aside class="guide-callout">
        <strong>Try this:</strong> click the e at position 2, then the e at position 3. One becomes
        A and the other L with shifts 7 and 22. Then change the second shift to 7. What familiar
        cipher have you returned to? Set the second shift back to 22 before continuing.
      </aside>

      <h2 id="key">A key is a repeating set of instructions</h2>
      <p>
        Writing a long list of numbers is awkward. We can record each shift with a letter: A means
        no shift, B means 1, C means 2, and so on. We start at zero because shifting by zero leaves
        the letter alone.
      </p>
      <div class="key-number-reference" aria-label="Key letters and their shift numbers">
        {[...ABC].map((c, i) => (
          <span>
            <b>{c}</b>
            <small>{i}</small>
          </span>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p>
        So our two shifts, 7 and 22, can be written as <strong>HW</strong>. Repeat HW under the
        message and it reminds you which shift to use each time. HW need not be a word. A memorable
        word is often easier to remember, though: <strong>MAP</strong> records{" "}
        <strong>12, 0, 15</strong>.
      </p>
      <pre>message: m e e t a t t h e …
key:     M A P M A P M A P …
shift:  12 0 15 12 0 15 12 0 15 …</pre>
      <p>
        Now there are three shifts in the repeating cycle. The key length is 3. Letter 4 starts the
        key again at M. Spaces and punctuation do not use up a key letter in our tools.
      </p>
      <p>
        This repeating-key method is usually called the <strong>Vigenère cipher</strong>. Try MAP
        below. Click an input letter in the grid to follow its calculation, then change a key letter
        and see which message positions change.
      </p>
      <VigenereLab part="play" client:load />
      <p class="checkpoint">
        With MAP, “meet” begins YETF: m + 12 = Y, e + 0 = E, e + 15 = T, t + 12 wraps to F. Try key
        M to return to one Caesar shift. MAPMAP uses six written key letters but repeats the same
        three shifts, so its shortest period is still 3.
      </p>
      <aside class="guide-callout">
        <h3>A cipher for real secret messages</h3>
        <p>
          Spies, diplomats and soldiers all needed ways to keep messages secret. Vigenère-type
          systems were used for diplomatic correspondence and military messages, and versions of the
          cipher also appeared in agents’ materials. The name comes from Blaise de Vigenère, a
          French diplomat who wrote about ciphers in the 1500s.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a long time these changing alphabets defeated the simple frequency tricks that worked
          on Caesar, and the cipher gained a reputation for being unbreakable. That reputation
          outlasted the reality: Friedrich Kasiski published a general attack in{" "}
          <strong>1863</strong>. The point is not that it was magically safe, but that mixing the
          shifts hid the familiar clues. We are about to learn how to find them again.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2 id="patterns">Why do the frequency patterns become flatter?</h2>
      <p>
        Think about six e’s. A simple substitution puts all six into one new letter bar. With MAP
        repeated beneath them, those e’s become <code>Q E T Q E T</code>: two Q’s, two E’s and two
        T’s. One big pile has been shared between three smaller piles.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rest of the letters are shared out too. This tends to flatten the letter chart, so the
        usual English peaks no longer simply slide or swap labels. It will not always be perfectly
        flat: the key, the message and the amount of text all matter.
      </p>
      <p>
        Groups are affected for the same reason. THE starting against MAP becomes FHT. Against APM
        it becomes TWQ; against PMA it becomes ITE. A frequent group’s count can be split among
        several labels. The bigram and trigram charts therefore lose some of their strongest
        familiar peaks too.
      </p>
      <VigenereEffects client:load />
      <p>
        Suspect multiple shifts when one Caesar correction fails and neither the letter pattern nor
        a consistent relabelling of groups looks convincing. Combine these clues rather than
        deciding from one missing THE. If counting and IC are new,{" "}
        <a href={`${base}/guides/identify/#starter`}>start with the counting exercises</a>.
      </p>
      <aside class="guide-callout">
        <strong>A useful plan:</strong> first look for a way into the key. A guessed word can
        suggest key letters. Repeated groups can suggest key lengths. IC can help compare those
        lengths. Once the length is plausible, separate the shifts and tackle ordinary Caesar
        ciphers again.
      </aside>

      <h2 id="crib">Method 1: what if we can guess a word?</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>crib</strong> is a word or phrase you think may be in the message. Perhaps the
        story tells you it is arranging a meeting. You might try MEET at the beginning. You are
        making a hypothesis, not deciding that it must be right.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our short practice ciphertext is <code>{short}</code>. Try writing MEET under its first four
        letters. To find an encoding shift, subtract the guessed plaintext letter’s number from the
        ciphertext letter’s number.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>Work out the first three possible key letters</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Cipher</th>
              <th>Guessed plain</th>
              <th>Shift</th>
              <th>Key letter</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Y = 24</td>
              <td>m = 12</td>
              <td>24 − 12 = 12</td>
              <td>M</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>E = 4</td>
              <td>e = 4</td>
              <td>4 − 4 = 0</td>
              <td>A</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>T = 19</td>
              <td>e = 4</td>
              <td>19 − 4 = 15</td>
              <td>P</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        That suggests MAP. If the key really has length 3, its fourth shift should repeat M. F = 5
        and t = 19, so 5 − 19 = −14. Wrap that result by adding 26: −14 + 26 = 12, which is M again.
        The fourth letter agrees with our guess.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Keep MEET in the crib box below and assumed key length 3. Check that the hypothesis is
          MAP.
        </li>
        <li>
          Read the rest of the decoded message. A readable whole instruction supports the guess much
          more than four convenient letters.
        </li>
        <li>
          Try assumed length 2. Now some crib letters demand different shifts in the same key
          position: that is a contradiction.
        </li>
        <li>
          Try moving the crib to another starting letter. If it stops making sense, the word,
          position or assumed key length may be wrong.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <VigenereLab part="crib" client:load />
      <p>
        A short crib can fit by chance, especially if it covers each key position only once. A
        longer crib gives more chances for contradictions. Keep an undoable record of your guesses
        and test them elsewhere before trusting the key.
      </p>

      <h2 id="kasiski">Method 2: follow a group that repeats</h2>
      <p>
        Imagine THE appearing twice. If it meets different key positions, it may encrypt
        differently. But if it meets the <em>same</em> part of the key both times, it gets the same
        shifts and makes the same ciphertext group.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a three-letter key, that happens after 3, 6, 9, 12… letters: a whole number of complete
        key cycles. So the distance between repeated group starts may be a multiple of the key
        length. Looking for those distances is called <strong>Kasiski examination</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        We made a short example from “THE CAT SAT THE CAT RAN THE CAT HID” with key MAP. Its
        repeated THE groups become FHT at letter positions 1, 10 and 19. Count from the start of one
        copy to the start of the next, not just the blank space between them.
      </p>
      <KasiskiMini client:load />
      <p>
        A gap of 9 could contain three cycles of a length-3 key, or one cycle of a length-9 key: 9 =
        3 × 3 = 1 × 9. Length 1 divides every gap, so it does not help narrow our search.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Find several repeated groups, usually 3 or more letters long.</li>
        <li>Write down their start positions and subtract to find gaps.</li>
        <li>
          Try lengths that divide several gaps exactly. A length explaining repeats in several
          different groups is more interesting than one accidental pair.
        </li>
        <li>
          Keep more than one candidate. Our gaps of 9 allow both 3 and 9; use the next method to
          help compare them.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Not every repeat comes from the same plaintext, and overlapping groups can describe one
        underlying repeat. Longer groups reduce some accidental matches, but may give fewer results.
        No repeats does not rule out Vigenère: you may need more ciphertext or a different way into
        the key.
      </p>

      <h2 id="ic">Method 3: which length brings the English pattern back?</h2>
      <p>
        Recall <strong>IC</strong>, the Index of Coincidence: the chance that two different
        positions you pick contain the same letter. English tends to use some letters much more than
        others, so a longer English passage often scores about <strong>0.066</strong>. Uniform
        random A–Z letters score about <strong>0.0385</strong>. Mixing shifts often spreads counts
        out and lowers the score.
      </p>
      <p>Suppose the key length is 3. Put every third ciphertext letter in the same pile:</p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>Separate a possible three-letter key into three streams</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Stream</th>
              <th>Message positions</th>
              <th>If the key is MAP</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th>1</th>
              <td>1, 4, 7, 10, …</td>
              <td>Every letter used M: shift 12</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>2</th>
              <td>2, 5, 8, 11, …</td>
              <td>Every letter used A: shift 0</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>3</th>
              <td>3, 6, 9, 12, …</td>
              <td>Every letter used P: shift 15</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Each stream is now a separate Caesar cipher. Caesar moves the letter labels but keeps
        matching pairs, so each stream’s IC should move back towards an English-like value, provided
        there is enough text. Calculate the three IC values and average them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now try length 2 instead. Positions 1, 3, 5, 7… actually used M, P, A, M… . We have mixed
        different shifts again, so the streams tend to stay flatter and score lower. We can compare
        several guessed lengths without knowing their key letters.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>Actual average-stream IC values for our longer MAP example</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Assumed length</th>
              <th>Average IC</th>
              <th>How to interpret it</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {[1, 2, 3, 6].map((n) => (
              <tr>
                <th>{n}</th>
                <td>{scores[n - 1].ic.toFixed(4)}</td>
                <td>
                  {n === 1
                    ? "Whole message: all three shifts mixed"
                    : n === 2
                      ? "Wrong grouping: still mixes the shifts"
                      : n === 3
                        ? "Promising: each stream uses one shift"
                        : "Also promising: a multiple of the true length"}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Length 6 can also score highly: its streams are smaller pieces of the length-3 streams, each
        still using only one shift. Try the <strong>shortest repeating length</strong> that gives a
        consistent readable result. A key written as MAPMAP behaves like MAP.
      </p>
      <p>
        Do not choose the tallest bar blindly. Very short streams can produce high scores by
        accident. Check their sizes, combine IC with repeated-group gaps, and finally try reading
        the message.
      </p>

      <h2 id="solve">Put the methods together on a complete example</h2>
      <p>
        The workshop below contains a longer original story. We encrypted it with a three-letter
        word. You can copy its ciphertext into the workbench or investigate it here.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Look at the repeated-group factors and IC chart. Select length 3 to prepare three unknown
          key letters, initially AAA (all shift zero).
        </li>
        <li>
          Select key position 1. Its letters all share one shift. Adjust that Caesar shift and
          compare the decoded stream’s letter chart with English. The suggested shifts are useful
          starting points, not answers you must accept.
        </li>
        <li>
          Try positions 2 and 3 in the same way. Watch the full decoded message after each change.
          Even a few recovered words can help you choose between close statistical guesses.
        </li>
        <li>
          If the message stays unreadable, revisit the length or the shifts. Reveal the worked
          solution after testing your own idea, then explain why its three shifts work together.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <VigenereLab part="break" client:load />
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}/tools/#vigenere`}>
          Investigate your own ciphertext ↗
        </a>
      </div>
      <aside class="source-notes">
        <h3>Sources and practice material</h3>
        <p>
          All teaching messages and calculations are original to this site. History:{" "}
          <a href="https://www.nsa.gov/portals/75/documents/news-features/declassified-documents/friedman-documents/lectures-speeches/FOLDER_175/41758839079798.pdf">
            William F. Friedman, Introduction to Cryptology IV, NSA Technical Journal (1961)
          </a>
          , especially printed pages 51 and 60, discusses diplomatic, military and agent use and
          Kasiski’s 1863 publication.{" "}
          <a href="https://www.nku.edu/~christensen/1901csscmat483%20section%2013%20vigenere%20cryptography.pdf">
            Chris Christensen’s Northern Kentucky University notes
          </a>{" "}
          discuss alternating alphabets, Vigenère’s diplomatic work and the cipher’s long-lived
          reputation. Method references:{" "}
          <a href="https://cs.uri.edu/cryptography/classical-ciphers/vigenere/cryptanalysis-article.html">
            University of Rhode Island
          </a>{" "}
          and <a href="https://www.cs.trincoll.edu/cryptography/vigenere.html">Trinity College</a>.
          English group comparisons use{" "}
          <a href="https://www.norvig.com/mayzner.html">Peter Norvig’s within-word counts</a>.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
